Burns
Acute Urgent Care
Blistering/Epidermal sloughing, Oedema, Carbonaceous sputum/Singed hair

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-021
Reason for Visit: Severe thermal injury with blistering and potential inhalation injury requiring immediate clinical assessment and stabilization.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who sustained a significant thermal burn to the face and upper torso following an accidental flash fire from a barbeque. The presence of facial burns and altered breathing necessitated an urgent, immediate face-to-face assessment to evaluate the airway for impending obstruction and initiate critical fluid resuscitation before the next available daytime clinic.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My chest and face are on fire, and my throat feels dry and scratchy when I breathe.
History of Present Illness: The patient sustained a thermal burn approximately 30 minutes ago when lighting an outdoor gas barbeque. The patient describes intense, burning pain across the anterior chest and face. Blistering began almost immediately on the chest. The spouse noted the patient coughed up dark, sooty material (carbonaceous sputum) shortly after the incident. The patient complains of a hoarse voice and a sensation of tightness in the throat, though currently able to speak in full sentences.
Functional Status: Ambulant, highly distressed, pacing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impending airway compromise is a critical, life-threatening risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient is in severe pain, anxious, with visible erythema and blistering over the anterior chest and lower face.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals partial-thickness burns (erythema with blistering and epidermal sloughing) covering approximately 15% of the Total Body Surface Area (TBSA), primarily on the anterior trunk. There is significant localized oedema. Facial exam reveals singed nasal hair, eyebrows, and carbonaceous sputum in the oropharynx, highly indicative of a thermal inhalation injury. Auscultation reveals mild expiratory wheeze. Tachycardia and tachypnoea are present. Expected investigations would include elevated carboxyhaemoglobin levels on arterial blood gas and hyponatraemia with hyperkalaemia on serum electrolytes due to fluid shifts.
Environmental Observations: The source of the fire is extinguished; the environment is safe for assessment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Applied cool water for 5 minutes prior to calling for help.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Burns - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Chemical Burn - Ruled out/unlikely due to the clear history of a gas explosion/flash fire rather than exposure to a corrosive liquid or powder.

Uncomplicated Sunburn - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of immediate blistering, singed facial hair, and the acute mechanism of injury.

Simple Upper Respiratory Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to the hoarse voice and wheeze occurring immediately after a blast of hot gas, pointing to thermal airway oedema rather than a viral etiology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits partial-thickness burns with the critical diagnostic tetrad components of blistering, oedema, singed facial hair, and carbonaceous sputum. This presentation strongly suggests a concomitant inhalation injury. An urgent after-hours home assessment was absolutely mandated because thermal airway oedema can progress to complete obstruction within hours. Delaying this assessment to wait for routine daytime hours would likely result in an unmanageable airway disaster and profound hypovolaemic shock. Immediate on-site triage allowed for rapid analgesia and the activation of emergency transport for prophylactic intubation and formal fluid resuscitation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Fentanyl 100mcg Intranasally for rapid, non-invasive analgesia. Covered burns loosely with clean, dry plastic wrap to reduce heat loss and pain from air currents. Immediately called 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport to a specialized Burn Center.
Positive Behavioral Change: By ensuring you always run cool (not ice-cold) running water over any burn for a full 20 minutes immediately after it happens, you draw the heat out of the deep tissues, which significantly reduces the final depth of the scar.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics; burn center management required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The spouse is instructed to monitor the patient's breathing continuously. If the patient develops harsh, noisy breathing (stridor) or becomes unresponsive, the spouse must update 000 immediately as the airway may be closing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Your facial burns suggest you may have breathed in hot gas, which can cause your throat to swell over the next few hours.
Clinical Course: The hospital team will likely place a breathing tube as a precaution before the swelling worsens, and they will give you large amounts of intravenous fluids to replace what is leaking into the burn blisters.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Sit completely upright to make breathing as easy as possible. Do not attempt to drink any water or swallow anything, as your airway is compromised.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I have covered the burns with plastic film to stop the air from hitting the exposed nerve endings, which drastically reduces the pain. The strong painkiller will take the edge off while we wait.
Actionable Steps: Try not to talk, as this stresses your vocal cords. Focus on taking slow, shallow breaths. Let your spouse gather your identification while we wait for the paramedics.

